"gist": "Stone houses, hearth and loom, communal fields, whisky stills, and the turning of the farming year.",
"text": "Homes in Stonetop are low and squat, built of Old Wall stone and thatched with barley straw, warmed by a central hearth. Most have a small cellar, a standing loom, a vegetable garden and pens for the goats, sheep, pigs and chickens that wander underfoot. Houses pass down the generations, big families spread into adjoining buildings, and newer homes go up just outside the Ringwall. There is no mill, so grain is ground by hand for flatbread, porridge, stew, ale and whisky mash. Water comes from the Cistern or rain caught in clay jars; washing is done down at the Stream, and rarely alone.\n\nClothes are leather, fur and handspun wool, hung with belts, pouches, torcs and brooches. Shoes are cut from a single piece of leather and laced up around the foot, with thick wool socks for winter boots.\n\nThe year turns on the fields: planting and kidding in spring, haymaking and berrying under the summer storms, the great harvest while the Wood blazes with colour in autumn, and long winters of trapping, butchering and tending the stills. Nearly every household distils whisky from barley mash, aged in jugs with toasted wood chips; it is the village's proudest export, and outsiders have never managed to copy it. After sundown the public house fills with drink, music and dancing.",
