"title": "The Foothills",
"cat": "The Vicinity",
"page": 144,
"gist": "Wooded hills two days north-west of Stonetop, where the Flats climb toward the Whitefangs; timber country.",
"text": "Two days north-west of Stonetop the Flats begin to buckle and rise, and the hills climb on until they meet the Whitefang Mountains. The Highway winds through them on its way to Barrier Pass, and to the east the hills curl around the edge of the Great Wood. It is rough, wooded country, threaded with streams and broken by gorges, with ponds and little lakes tucked between the ridges.\n\nOld stories make these hills a borderland, where the Rime Lords' reach once met the Green Lords', and later the farthest edge of the Barrow Builders' domain. Nobody lives here now. Stonetop comes for timber, and so do the folk of Barrier Pass. Village logging trips go in autumn once the harvest is in, with a wagon, two horses and a score of able hands, and they take the better part of two weeks. Loggers sometimes cross paths with patrols or hunting parties from the Pass.\n\nThe dangers most talked of are ordinary ones: losing the way, loose scree, flash floods in the ravines, sudden rain and snow. But hunters also speak of boars, cave bears, cougars and wolves, of stranger things among the old mounds, and of Fae and wild spirits who do not care for company.",
